# Project: Jest JavaScript Unit Test Framework

## Framework Structure
- Use Jest as the primary test runner for JavaScript/TypeScript projects
- Organize tests in __tests__ directories or co-locate with source files using .test.js/.spec.js suffix
- Use TypeScript with ts-jest or Babel for transpilation
- Configure jest.config.js (or jest.config.ts) as the single source of truth for test settings
- Leverage Jest's built-in code coverage, mocking, and snapshot capabilities

## Coding Standards
- Follow JavaScript/TypeScript best practices
- Use meaningful, descriptive test and variable names
- Implement clean code principles
- Limit describe block nesting to 3 levels maximum
- Keep each test file focused on a single module or component
- Prefer async/await over Promise chains in asynchronous tests

## Test Organization
- Group related tests using describe blocks
- Use it/test functions for individual test cases
- Implement beforeEach/afterEach for per-test setup and teardown
- Use beforeAll/afterAll only for expensive shared resources that are truly read-only
- Separate unit, integration, and e2e tests into distinct directories

## Best Practices
- Follow Arrange-Act-Assert (AAA) pattern consistently
- Write focused, single-responsibility tests
- Avoid test interdependencies and shared mutable state between tests
- Use jest.fn() for creating mock functions
- Clear or reset mocks in afterEach to prevent cross-test pollution
- Prefer toEqual for deep equality checks over toBe for objects
- Prefer explicit assertions over implicit pass-by-not-throwing

## Mocking Strategies
- Use jest.mock() for module-level mocking with automatic hoisting
- Implement jest.spyOn() for method-level spying without replacing the full module
- Use jest.useFakeTimers() with modern mode for timer-dependent tests
- Mock HTTP requests with msw (Mock Service Worker) for realistic network simulation
- Use manual mocks in __mocks__ directories for complex or frequently used modules
- Restore all mocks after each test with jest.restoreAllMocks() or clearMocks: true in config

## Assertions
- Use expect() with specific, descriptive matchers
- Implement custom matchers with expect.extend() for domain-specific assertions
- Use toMatchSnapshot() only for stable, intentional snapshot testing
- Use expect.assertions(n) in async tests to ensure all assertions run
- Prefer specific matchers (toHaveBeenCalledWith) over generic ones (toBeTruthy)
- Use toThrow and toThrowError with specific error messages or types

## Coverage
- Configure coverage with the v8 provider (recommended for accuracy) via coverageProvider
- Set meaningful thresholds in coverageThreshold for statements, branches, functions, and lines
- Use collectCoverageFrom to include all source files, not just tested ones
- Exclude generated files, configuration, and index barrel files from coverage
- Generate lcov reports for CI integration and HTML reports for local review
- Run coverage in CI but not in watch mode to keep local feedback fast

## Performance
- Use --runInBand only for debugging; prefer parallel workers in CI
- Configure maxWorkers in jest.config to balance CPU usage in CI environments
- Use testPathPattern or testNamePattern to run targeted subsets during development
- Use jest --onlyFailures (or -f) to rerun only failing tests during development
- Use --bail to stop early on first failure in pre-commit hooks
- Minimize unnecessary setup/teardown operations to reduce suite runtime

## Snapshot Testing
- Keep snapshots small and focused on the critical output structure
- Commit snapshots to version control alongside their test files
- Update snapshots deliberately with jest --updateSnapshot (-u) after intentional changes
- Use inline snapshots (toMatchInlineSnapshot) for small, readable, self-documenting outputs
- Avoid snapshotting highly dynamic content (timestamps, random IDs)
- Delete obsolete snapshots with --ci flag to prevent stale files from accumulating

## Test Data Management
- Use factory functions or fixtures for reusable, composable test data
- Define TypeScript types or JSDoc types for all test data structures
- Avoid hardcoded magic values; use named constants or enums
- Generate realistic dynamic data with @faker-js/faker when needed
- Keep test data close to the test that uses it; share only when truly common

## Configuration
- Use jest.config.js (or .ts) for maintainability over package.json config
- Configure moduleNameMapper for path aliases and static asset mocking
- Use projects in jest.config.js to run multiple configurations (e.g., unit vs. integration)
- Set testEnvironment to node for backend code and jsdom for browser/DOM code
- Use globalSetup and globalTeardown for one-time infrastructure setup (databases, servers)

## CI/CD Integration
- Use --ci flag in CI environments to disable interactive prompts and fail on missing snapshots
- Configure --reporters with jest-junit for JUnit XML output for CI pipeline compatibility
- Enable test result caching with --cache for faster repeated runs
- Upload coverage reports to services like Codecov or SonarCloud
- Run tests with --forceExit if integration tests may leave open handles
- Run linting and type-checking in parallel with the test suite, not sequentially

## TypeScript Integration
- Use ts-jest or @swc/jest for TypeScript transpilation in Jest
- Configure paths in tsconfig to match moduleNameMapper in jest.config
- Enable diagnostics: false in ts-jest config only when TS errors are handled elsewhere
- Keep a dedicated tsconfig.test.json for test-specific TypeScript settings

## Error Handling
- Test both success and failure code paths explicitly
- Assert on specific error messages and types using toThrow and toThrowError
- Use expect(fn).rejects.toThrow() for async error assertions
- Use expect.assertions(n) in async tests to catch silent resolution instead of rejection
- Verify error boundaries and fallback behavior in component tests

## Documentation
- Document complex test scenarios with brief inline comments explaining the why
- Maintain a TESTING.md describing conventions, patterns, and how to run tests
- Include examples for any custom matchers defined in the project
- Document project-specific mock strategies and shared fixture patterns

Remember to keep tests fast, deterministic, and independent — and leverage Jest's rich ecosystem of plugins and its deep integration with Babel and TypeScript toolchains for a reliable unit testing experience.
